page("first-setup-step-by-step","Setting Up a Collapsible Grill: First Setup, Step by Step","The exact assembly order for a tab-and-slot collapsible charcoal grill, where to put it, and how to take it back down, using the Slot Grill.","c13",f"""
<p>The first time you put a flat-pack grill together it takes a few minutes of looking at the parts. By the third time it is muscle memory. This is the order Slot Grill gives on its <a href="{SPECS}">specs, setup and care page</a>, with notes from the point of view of someone doing it for the first time.</p>
<h2>Before you start</h2>{ul(["Pick a stable, level, non-combustible spot: bare soil, gravel, sand, concrete or a fire-safe pad, clear of structures, vehicles, tents, dry grass and anything that can burn.","Lay the parts out flat. You have two end pieces, two side panels (one with two tabs at each end, one with a single tab at each top end and three tabs along the bottom), two grates, and griddle plates if you bought them.","Gloves are handy: cut steel edges are clean but firm."])}
<h2>Assembly</h2>{ol(["<b>Start with one end piece</b>, SLOT GRILL text facing out.","<b>Add the two-tab side panel.</b> Insert the two tabs on one end of it into the slots on the end piece.","<b>Add the second end piece.</b> Text facing out, slide the other two tabs of the side panel into it. The end pieces are universal, so either works on either end as long as the text faces out.","<b>Lock in the V.</b> Take the remaining side panel. Set its top tabs onto the top of each end piece, then slide its three bottom tabs into the bottom of the first side panel. The sides interlock into a tight V.","<b>Check it.</b> Every tab fully seated, grill level.","<b>Add fuel</b>, charcoal or cooking wood, and light it (see {0}).".format(L("lighting-charcoal-in-a-flat-pack-grill","lighting charcoal")),"<b>Set the grates</b> on top, or swap one half for a griddle plate."])}
{tip("Do the first assembly at home, cold, on the kitchen floor or a driveway. Doing it once without a hungry crowd watching makes the first trip easy.")}
<h2>Taking it down</h2>{ol(["Let the grill and ash cool completely. This takes longer than you think; give it time.","Empty the ash into a metal container (see {0}).".format(L("charcoal-ash-handling-and-disposal","ash handling")),"Lift off the grates or griddles.","Slide the V side panel out, then pull the end pieces off the other side panel.","Stack the flat pieces with the grates and griddles."])}
<p>The Slot Grill also comes with two grate keys that double as bottle openers, according to the specs page's what's-in-the-box list.</p>
""","setup",[("How long does a Slot Grill take to set up?","About 30 to 60 seconds once you've done it a couple of times, according to Slot Grill."),("Which way do the end pieces go?","Either end works; just keep the SLOT GRILL text facing out.")])

page("first-cook-on-a-collapsible-grill","Your First Cook on a Collapsible Grill","A low-stress plan for the first fire on a new flat-pack charcoal grill: what to cook, how much charcoal, and what to watch for.","c28",f"""
<p>The first cook on a new grill should be forgiving. Pick food that tolerates a little extra heat, use less charcoal than you think, and leave yourself room to move food around. Here is a plan that works.</p>
<h2>Before the day</h2>{ul(["Season the grill first: {0}.".format(L("seasoning-a-new-collapsible-grill","how to season a new collapsible grill")),"Assemble it once at home so you know the order.","Have a chimney starter, charcoal, long tongs, a spatula, heat-safe gloves and an <a href='{0}'>instant-read thermometer</a>.".format(THERMO)])}
<h2>The menu</h2><p>Bratwurst or sausages (fully cooked or fresh), thick-cut vegetables, and something sweet like grilled pineapple. Sausages teach you heat zones, vegetables teach you timing, fruit teaches you how fast sugar burns. Burgers are fine too; see {L("smash-burgers-on-a-griddle-plate","smash burgers on the griddle")}.</p>
<h2>The fire</h2>{ol(["Fill a chimney about half full for the Compact, a bit more for the Full Size.","When the top coals are covered in light gray ash, pour them toward one end of the firebox.","Leave the other end empty or nearly so. That is your safe zone.","Set the grates and let them heat for a few minutes, then scrape and oil them."])}
<h2>The cook</h2>{ol(["Start sausages over the coals to brown, then move them to the empty side to finish.","Lay vegetables on the hot side and turn when marked.","Check sausages: fresh pork or beef sausage to 160°F, fresh poultry sausage to 165°F, precooked to 165°F.","Grill pineapple last, over the hot side, a minute or two per side."])}
<h2>What to notice</h2><p>How fast food browns over the coals. How much cooler the empty side is. How the fire changes after 30 minutes. That's everything you need to know to plan the next cook.</p>
""","setup",[("How much charcoal should I use the first time?","Start with about half a chimney on the Compact and a little more on the Full Size. You can always add lit coals.")])

page("lighting-charcoal-in-a-flat-pack-grill","Lighting Charcoal in a Flat-Pack Grill","Chimney starters, fire starters and other clean ways to light charcoal in a collapsible grill, and why to skip lighter fluid.","c28",f"""
<p>A collapsible grill has a shallow, open firebox, which is great for airflow and easy lighting. Here are the methods that work best, in order of preference.</p>
<h2>1. Chimney starter (best)</h2>{ol(["Crumple two sheets of newspaper (or use two natural fire-starter cubes) under the chimney.","Fill the chimney with charcoal: about half for the Compact, more for the Full Size or a big cook.","Light the paper. Set the chimney on a non-combustible surface, or right in the grill's firebox.","Wait until the top coals have a light coat of gray ash, usually 15 to 20 minutes.","Wearing gloves, pour the coals where you want them."])}
<h2>2. Fire starters in the grill</h2><p>Build a small pyramid of charcoal around two or three natural starter cubes directly in the firebox. Light the cubes. When the coals are ashed over, spread them into your zones. Slower than a chimney but nothing extra to pack.</p>
<h2>3. Kindling and wood</h2><p>Both Slot Grill models burn clean cooking wood, so you can start a small kindling fire and add charcoal or splits on top. See {L("cooking-with-wood-in-a-collapsible-grill","cooking with wood")}.</p>
<h2>Why skip lighter fluid</h2><p>Lighter fluid can leave a taste if food goes on too soon, and squirting it near a lit fire is dangerous. Never add lighter fluid to coals that are already lit. Never use gasoline.</p>
<h2>Lump vs briquettes</h2>{table(["","Lump charcoal","Briquettes"],[["Lights","Faster","Slower"],["Burns","Hotter, faster, less even","Steadier and longer"],["Ash","Less","More"],["Best for","Searing, quick cooks","Longer cooks, steady heat"]])}
""","setup",[("How long until charcoal is ready?","Usually 15 to 20 minutes in a chimney, when the top coals have a light coat of gray ash.")])

page("two-zone-fire-collapsible-grill","How to Build a Two-Zone Fire in a Collapsible Grill","The single most useful fire layout for a lidless flat-pack grill: hot side for searing, cool side for finishing, and how to size it.","c08",f"""
<p>A two-zone fire is coals on one side and few or none on the other. It is how you control heat on a grill with no knobs and no lid. On a collapsible grill like the <a href="{COMPACT}">Slot Grill</a>, it is the layout you'll use most.</p>
<h2>How to build it</h2>{ol(["Light your charcoal and wait for it to ash over.","Pour it into one end of the firebox, roughly two-thirds of the length.","Bank it deeper toward the far end if you want a very hot spot.","Leave the remaining third empty or with a thin scatter of coals.","Set and preheat the grates over the whole grill."])}
<h2>How to use it</h2>{table(["Zone","Use it for"],[["Hot (over deep coals)","Searing steaks, chops, burgers; browning sausages; charring peppers"],["Medium (edge of the coals)","Chicken pieces, thicker vegetables, kebabs"],["Cool (no coals)","Finishing thick cuts, holding cooked food, escaping flare-ups, warming buns"]])}
<h2>Sizing for each grill</h2><p>The Compact's cooking area is 16.25&quot; × 9&quot;, so the cool zone is about a 5&quot; strip at one end. The Full Size gives you 20.5&quot; × 11.25&quot;, enough for a generous cool zone or even three zones (hot, medium, cool).</p>
<h2>Half grate, half griddle</h2><p>The Slot Grill's two-piece top lets you put a <a href="{GRID}">griddle plate</a> on one half. Put the griddle over the hotter coals for smash burgers or on the cooler end for eggs, onions or toasting buns. See {L("cooking-on-griddle-plates","cooking on the griddle plates")}.</p>
""","setup",[("Why is a two-zone fire important on a lidless grill?","It gives you somewhere to move food when it's browning too fast, and a gentler zone to finish thick cuts without burning the outside.")])

page("adding-charcoal-during-a-cook","Adding Charcoal During a Long Cook","How to keep a collapsible grill going for longer cooks: when to add fuel, lit vs unlit coals, and how to do it safely.","full13",f"""
<p>Charcoal burns down. In a shallow open firebox, a full chimney gives you a good hot window, then steadily less heat. For quick grilling that never matters. For a crowd, a long evening or a fire you'll keep for warmth afterward, you'll need to feed it.</p>
<h2>Signs it's time</h2>{ul(["Food that browned in 2 minutes now takes 4.","Coals are mostly gray-white with little glow.","You can hold your hand over the grate longer than before (keep it well above the grate)."])}
<h2>Lit coals vs unlit</h2>{table(["Method","Pros","Cons"],[["Add pre-lit coals from a chimney","Heat returns fast, no smoke from fresh charcoal","Need a second chimney or space to light"],["Add unlit coals to the edge of the fire","Simple","Smoky and a dip in heat for 10–15 minutes"]])}
<h2>Getting to the coals</h2><p>Slot Grill's Full Size page notes its two-piece grates sit inside the body, so you can tilt one up to add charcoal without your food sliding off. On either model, move food to the other half, lift the empty half with gloves or the grate key, add fuel and set it back.</p>
<h2>Safety</h2>{ul(["Never add lighter fluid to a lit fire.","Use long tongs and gloves.","Set hot grates down only on a non-combustible surface."])}
""","setup",[("Can I add charcoal without removing the food?","Yes. Slide food to one half, lift the other grate half, add coals, and set it back.")])

page("cooking-with-wood-in-a-collapsible-grill","Cooking With Wood in a Collapsible Grill","Using clean cooking wood instead of (or with) charcoal in a flat-pack grill: which woods, how to burn down to coals, and how to cook over them.","c28",f"""
<p>Both Slot Grill models are built to burn charcoal or clean cooking wood. Wood-fired cooking takes a little more patience, but it is a great skill at camp where wood is allowed and plentiful.</p>
<h2>Good wood</h2>{ul(["Seasoned (dry) hardwoods: oak, hickory, pecan, mesquite, fruitwoods like apple or cherry.","Split small, wrist-thick or smaller, to fit the firebox and burn down evenly.","Dry: it should sound hollow when knocked together and show cracks at the ends."])}
<h2>Avoid</h2>{ul(["Treated, painted or stained lumber, pallets and plywood.","Green (wet) wood: smoky and sputtering.","Resinous softwoods like pine for cooking; they leave soot and bitter flavor.","Any wood from outside the area where firewood transport rules apply. Buy it where you burn it."])}
<h2>Cook on coals, not flames</h2>{ol(["Build a small kindling fire in the firebox.","Add splits a few at a time.","Let them burn down to glowing coals, 30 to 45 minutes.","Rake coals into a two-zone layout ({0}).".format(L("two-zone-fire-collapsible-grill","how")),"Keep a split burning at the far end to make fresh coals and rake them over as needed."])}
<h2>Wood plus charcoal</h2><p>An easy hybrid: a charcoal base for steady heat plus a small chunk of hardwood on top for flavor. You get the convenience of charcoal with some smoke.</p>
""","setup",[("Can I burn wood in a Slot Grill?","Yes. Both models burn charcoal or cooking wood. Follow local fire restrictions.")])

page("cooking-on-griddle-plates","Cooking on Griddle Plates on a Collapsible Grill","How to use flat steel griddle (plancha) plates on a collapsible charcoal grill: heat, oil, what to cook, and cleanup.","grid1",f"""
<p>A griddle plate turns a charcoal grill into a flat top. The <a href="{GRID}">Slot Grill Griddle Add-On</a> is carbon steel made for the grill's two-piece top: run one plate and one grate, or two plates for a full flat top, on either the Compact or the Full Size. The plates stack flat with the grill for travel.</p>
<h2>What griddle plates are best at</h2>{ul(["Smash burgers and crispy-edged patties","Breakfast: bacon, eggs, pancakes, hash browns","Fajitas and sliced vegetables","Small foods that fall through grates: shrimp, chopped onions, mushrooms","Toasting buns and tortillas"])}
<h2>Heat and oil</h2>{ol(["Season the plates like the grill ({0}).".format(L("seasoning-a-new-collapsible-grill","how")),"Preheat over coals for several minutes.","Check heat: a few drops of water should skitter and evaporate quickly.","Add a thin film of high-smoke-point oil right before the food.","Use the coal layout under the plate to make hot and cool areas."])}
<h2>Cleanup</h2><p>While still warm (not scorching), scrape with a flat metal scraper. Wipe with a paper towel. If something is stuck, a little water on the warm plate loosens it; scrape and dry. Finish with a thin coat of oil. Let it cool before stacking.</p>
""","setup",[("Can I run a grate and a griddle at once?","Yes. The two-piece top takes one griddle plate and one grate, or two plates.")])

page("cast-iron-on-a-collapsible-grill","Using Cast Iron on a Collapsible Grill","How to cook with cast iron skillets and pots on a flat-pack charcoal grill: weight, heat and safe handling.","full08",f"""
<p>Cast iron and charcoal are a great pair. Slot Grill states both models are strong enough for cast iron, and its specs page says the Full Size gives room for cast iron and bigger cuts. Here is how to do it well.</p>
<h2>Sizing</h2><p>Use a pan that sits stably across the grates without hanging past the edges. A 10&quot; or 12&quot; skillet suits the Compact's 16.25&quot; × 9&quot; area; the Full Size (20.5&quot; × 11.25&quot;) handles bigger skillets and a Dutch oven. The Slot Grill FAQ describes the Full Size as handling appropriately sized cast iron.</p>
<h2>Technique</h2>{ul(["Set the grill level first, then add the pan, then the food.","Preheat cast iron gradually over medium coals to avoid hot spots.","Use the two-zone layout: pan over coals for searing, pan moved to the cool side for simmering.","Remember a full Dutch oven is heavy and hot; plan where it'll go when it comes off."])}
<h2>Good cast iron cooks</h2>{ul(["Skillet cornbread or biscuits (on the cool side with coals around it)","Chili or beans simmered at camp","Seared steak with pan butter","Breakfast potatoes and peppers"])}
<h2>Safety</h2><p>Thick gloves, a stable surface, and kids well back. Never move the grill while it is loaded and lit.</p>
""","setup",[("Is a Slot Grill strong enough for cast iron?","Slot Grill states both models are strong enough for cast iron. Use appropriately sized pans that sit stably on the grates.")])

page("using-a-collapsible-grill-as-a-fire-pit","Using a Collapsible Grill as a Fire Pit After Dinner","How a flat-pack charcoal grill doubles as a small fire pit after the cook, and the rules to follow.","c28",f"""
<p>After dinner, a lot of owners just keep the fire going. Slot Grill's FAQ says the steel is thick and durable enough to handle fire pit heat, so you can cook on it and then hang out around it. Here's how to do it responsibly.</p>
<h2>How</h2>{ol(["Finish cooking and clear the grates. Lift them off with gloves once they've cooled a bit, or leave them on as a spark screen.","Add small splits of dry hardwood a few at a time.","Keep the fire small. It's a compact firebox; a towering fire is the wrong idea.","Let it burn down completely before bed. Drown and stir, or let coals burn to cold ash where water is scarce."])}
<h2>Rules first</h2>{ul(["Only where campfires are allowed, and never during a burn ban.","Stable, non-combustible ground, well clear of tents, vehicles and trees.","Water or an extinguisher at hand. Never leave it unattended."])}
<h2>S'mores and dessert</h2><p>Toast marshmallows over coals rather than flames. Grilled peaches or pineapple on the last coals are another good finish; see {L("grilled-fruit-dessert-collapsible-grill","grilled fruit")}.</p>
""","setup",[("Can the Slot Grill be used as a fire pit?","Yes, according to Slot Grill. Use it where fires are allowed, on a stable non-combustible surface, and never leave it unattended.")])

page("wind-and-weather-collapsible-grill","Cooking in Wind, Cold and Rain on a Collapsible Grill","How wind, cold and wet weather change a charcoal fire in an open flat-pack grill and what to do about each.","both02",f"""
<p>An open firebox is more exposed to weather than a lidded cooker. The fix is mostly planning.</p>
{table(["Condition","Effect","What to do"],[["Wind","Coals burn hot and fast on the windward side; sparks and ash blow","Shelter the grill (with safe clearance), point the coal end into the wind, use more fuel, skip cooking in high wind or red-flag conditions"],["Cold","Food and steel soak up heat; slower cooks","More charcoal, keep coals together, trust the thermometer"],["Rain","Hard to light, coals weaken","Keep charcoal dry in a lidded bin; cook in the open, never under a tent, awning or roof; dry and oil the grill after"],["Hot, dry weather","Spark risk","Check burn bans, clear the area, keep water at hand"]])}
<h2>Never move the fire indoors</h2><p>Burning charcoal produces carbon monoxide. Never use a charcoal grill inside a tent, camper, garage, vehicle, boat cabin or any enclosed space, even to get out of the rain.</p>
<h2>After a wet cook</h2><p>Carbon steel left wet will rust. Dry the grill (a warm grill dries itself quickly), wipe on a thin coat of oil, and store it dry. See {L("surface-rust-on-carbon-steel","surface rust")}.</p>
""","setup",[("Can I use a charcoal grill under a tent or awning in the rain?","No. Charcoal produces carbon monoxide and the fire is a hazard. Cook in the open.")])
